  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "fbf1b0c2",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Comparing GD-learned weights with the hand-coded W\\*\n",
    "\n",
    "**Why we can't compare raw weights.** Each attention block has a *gauge freedom*: the scores depend on `(W_q, W_k)` only through `M_QK = W_qᵀ W_k`, and the value path depends on `(W_v, W_o)` only through `M_OV = W_o W_v`. Any invertible `R` with `W_q → R W_q`, `W_k → R⁻ᵀ W_k` leaves the computed function unchanged — so GD's `W_q` need not resemble the hand-coded one even when both networks compute the **same map**. Entry-wise weight comparison is therefore meaningless.\n",
    "\n",
    "We instead compare **gauge-invariant** quantities, in the *fixed, interpretable* coordinates (input = token[0:20] ⊕ position[0:200]; output = vocab):\n",
    "\n",
    "1. **Functional** — does GD's predictive distribution equal the count-α one? Per-threshold `KL(GD ‖ ctx-count)` vs `KL(GD ‖ W*)`.\n",
    "2. **Behavioral** — does GD implement the induction circuit? Layer-1 = previous-position head; Layer-2 = attend to keys whose *previous* token equals the current token, then copy.\n",
    "3. **Structural** — `M_QK⁽¹⁾` in token/position coordinates, and the signal-token **permutation symmetry** the task demands (a correct solution must be S₁₀-equivariant over the signal tokens).\n",
    "\n",
    "> Run the evaluation cell above first — it defines `device`, `load_data`, `load_model`, `PerfectInductionTransformer`, `thresholds`, `TOKEN_START/END`, `WS_SIGMA`, `N_SEQS`."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 8,
